<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-arithmetic">
  <title>Operations</title>
  <objectives xml:id="li-number-operations"><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol>
      <li><p>Use the four arithmetic operations to solve problems.</p></li>
      <li><p>Determine whether each operation is commutative and associative.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain when multiplication or division distributes over a grouped sum or difference.</p></li>
    </ol></objectives>
  <introduction><p>Addition, subtraction, multiplication and division each have their own properties. Brackets and other grouping symbols reveal the structure of an expression.</p></introduction>

  <section xml:id="arithmetic-intro">
    <title>Operations and Their Properties</title>
    <p>Four operations. Four familiar ideas.</p>
    <table><title>The four operations</title><tabular><row header="yes"><cell>Operation</cell><cell>Think of</cell><cell>Example</cell></row><row><cell>Addition</cell><cell>Putting together</cell><cell><m>3+5=8</m></cell></row><row><cell>Subtraction</cell><cell>Taking away</cell><cell><m>8-3=5</m></cell></row><row><cell>Multiplication</cell><cell>Equal groups</cell><cell><m>3\times5=15</m></cell></row><row><cell>Division</cell><cell>Sharing equally</cell><cell><m>15\div3=5</m></cell></row></tabular></table>
    <p>Try swapping the numbers. Does the answer change?</p>
    <table xml:id="frayer-commutative"><title>Commutative — swap the numbers</title><tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor"><col width="50%" right="minor"/><col width="50%"/><row bottom="minor"><cell><line><em>Definition</em></line><line>Swap the numbers.</line><line>The answer always stays the same.</line></cell><cell><line><em>Key features</em></line><line>Order changes.</line><line><m>a+b=b+a</m></line><line><m>a\times b=b\times a</m></line></cell></row><row><cell><line><em>Examples</em></line><line><m>3+5=5+3</m></line><line><m>4\times7=7\times4</m></line></cell><cell><line><em>Non-examples</em></line><line><m>8-3\ne3-8</m></line><line><m>12\div4\ne4\div12</m></line></cell></row></tabular></table>
    <p>Keep the numbers in the same order. Move the brackets instead.</p>
    <table xml:id="frayer-associative"><title>Associative — move the brackets</title><tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor"><col width="50%" right="minor"/><col width="50%"/><row bottom="minor"><cell><line><em>Definition</em></line><line>Change which numbers are grouped.</line><line>The answer always stays the same.</line></cell><cell><line><em>Key features</em></line><line>Order stays the same.</line><line>Only the grouping changes.</line><line><m>(a+b)+c=a+(b+c)</m></line></cell></row><row><cell><line><em>Examples</em></line><line><m>(2+3)+4=2+(3+4)</m></line><line><m>(2\times3)\times4</m></line><line><m>=2\times(3\times4)</m></line></cell><cell><line><em>Non-examples</em></line><line><m>(12-6)-2\ne12-(6-2)</m></line><line><m>(24\div6)\div2</m></line><line><m>\ne24\div(6\div2)</m></line></cell></row></tabular></table>
    <p>Multiply everything inside the brackets.</p>
    <table xml:id="frayer-distributive"><title>Distributive law — multiply each term</title><tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor"><col width="50%" right="minor"/><col width="50%"/><row bottom="minor"><cell><line><em>Definition</em></line><line>Multiply each term inside the brackets.</line></cell><cell><line><em>Key features</em></line><line>Two operations work together.</line><line><m>a(b+c)=ab+ac</m></line><line><m>a(b-c)=ab-ac</m></line></cell></row><row><cell><line><em>Examples</em></line><line><m>3(4+5)=3\times4+3\times5</m></line><line><m>2(7-3)=2\times7-2\times3</m></line></cell><cell><line><em>Non-examples</em></line><line><m>3(4+5)\ne3\times4+5</m></line><line><m>2(7-3)\ne2\times7-3</m></line></cell></row></tabular></table>
    <p><em>Connect across:</em> <m>3(4+5)=12+15=27</m>. The same idea lets us expand <m>3(x+5)=3x+15</m> in algebra.</p>
    <p><em>Division:</em> split the top of a fraction, not the bottom.</p>
    <me>\frac{12+6}{3}=\frac{12}{3}+\frac{6}{3}=6</me>
    <me>\frac{12}{2+4}=2\ne\frac{12}{2}+\frac{12}{4}=9</me>
    <p>The divisor must not be zero.</p>
    <p><em>Quick summary:</em> swapping is not regrouping.</p>
    <table xml:id="operation-properties">
      <title>Properties of the four operations</title>
      <tabular>
        <col width="23%"/><col width="23%"/><col width="23%"/><col width="31%"/>
        <row header="yes"><cell>Operation</cell><cell>Commutative?</cell><cell>Associative?</cell><cell>Distributive relationship</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Addition</cell><cell>Yes</cell><cell>Yes</cell><cell>—</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Subtraction</cell><cell>No</cell><cell>No</cell><cell>—</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Multiplication</cell><cell>Yes</cell><cell>Yes</cell><cell>Over addition and subtraction</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Division</cell><cell>No</cell><cell>No</cell><cell>Over sums or differences in the numerator</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="arithmetic-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-arithmetic">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Evaluate 7+3×4.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>Multiplication is performed first: 7+12=19.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-arithmetic">
      <statement><p>Evaluate (7+3)×4 and explain why it differs.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The brackets group the addition first, giving 10×4=40.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
